\fs96 \cf2 Unsafe pointers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 UnsafePointer}} type is one of several pointer types available in the standard library to indirectly reference locations in memory.\
You can use an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to dynamically allocate and free memory, or to point to memory allocated by some other piece of code. You can use these pointers to write code that interacts with low-level interfaces, to interface with other programming languages, or to build array-like data structures. But as the name suggests, they're inherently unsafe. For example, when using unsafe pointers, you're responsible for ensuring that memory gets allocated and freed correctly.\
In general, you should prefer safe pointer types when possible, reserving 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for those use cases where no other pointer type works. For a comparison of standard library pointer types, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/pointers/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Intro to pointers}}.\
:::\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Unsafe pointer basics\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 An 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a type that holds an address to memory. You can store and retrieve values in that memory. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type is generic\'97it can point to any type of value, and the value type is specified as a parameter. The value pointed to by a pointer is sometimes called a pointee.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # Allocate memory to hold a value\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  ptr \cf5 =\cf14  alloc\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ](\cf15 1\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Initialize the allocated memory\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb13 ptr\cf5 .\cf14 init_pointee_copy\cf5 (\cf15 100\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
