\fs64 \cf2 Lifecycle of a pointer\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 At any given time, a pointer can be in one of several states:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Uninitialized. Just like any variable, a variable of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can be declared but uninitialized.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  ptr\cf5 :\cf14  UnsafePointer\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ,\cf14  MutExternalOrigin\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Null. A null pointer has an address of 0, indicating an invalid pointer.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 ptr \cf5 =\cf14  \cf5 \{\}\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Pointing to allocated, uninitialized memory. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 alloc()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function returns a pointer to a newly-allocated block of memory with space for the specified number of elements of the pointee's type.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 ptr \cf5 =\cf14  alloc\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ](\cf15 1\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 Trying to dereference a pointer to uninitialized memory results in undefined behavior.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Pointing to initialized memory. You can initialize an allocated, uninitialized pointer by moving or copying an existing value into the memory. Or you can get a pointer to an existing value by calling the constructor with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 to
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword argument.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 ptr\cf5 .\cf14 init_pointee_copy\cf5 (\cf14 value\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf6 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
# or\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
ptr\cf5 .\cf14 init_pointee_move\cf5 (\cf14 value\cf5 ^)\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf6 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
# or\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
ptr \cf5 =\cf14  UnsafePointer\cf5 (\cf14 to\cf5 =\cf14 value\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 Once the value is initialized, you can read or mutate it using the dereference syntax:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  oldValue \cf5 =\cf14  ptr\cf5 []\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
ptr\cf5 []\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  newValue\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Dangling. When you free the pointer's allocated memory, you're left with a dangling pointer. The address still points to its previous location, but the memory is no longer allocated to this pointer. Trying to dereference the pointer, or calling any method that would access the memory location results in undefined behavior.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 ptr\cf5 .\cf14 free\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
