\fs48 \cf2 Allocating memory\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 alloc()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function to allocate memory. This function returns a new pointer pointing to the requested memory. You can allocate space for one or more values of the pointee's type.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  ptr \cf5 =\cf14  alloc\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ](\cf15 10\cf5 )\cf14  \cf6 # Allocate space for 10 Int values\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The allocated space is uninitialized\'97like a variable that's been declared but not initialized.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Initializing the pointee\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To initialize allocated memory, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  provides the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/#init_pointee_copy"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul init_pointee_copy()}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/#init_pointee_move"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul init_pointee_move()}} methods. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 ptr\cf5 .\cf14 init_pointee_copy\cf5 (\cf14 my_value\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To move a value into the pointer's memory location, use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 init_pointee_move()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 str_ptr\cf5 .\cf14 init_pointee_move\cf5 (\cf14 my_string\cf5 ^)\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Note that to move the value, you usually need to add the transfer sigil (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), unless the value is a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/#register-passable"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul trivial type}} (like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) or a newly-constructed, "owned" value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 str_ptr\cf5 .\cf14 init_pointee_move\cf5 (\cf18 "Owned string"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Alternately, you can get a pointer to an existing value by calling the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor with the keyword 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 to
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument. This is useful for getting a pointer to a value on the stack, for example.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  counter\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 5\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  ptr \cf5 =\cf14  UnsafePointer\cf5 (\cf14 to\cf5 =\cf14 counter\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Note that when calling 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer(to=value)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you don't need to allocate memory, since you're pointing to an existing value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Dereferencing pointers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 []
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  dereference operator to access the value stored at a pointer (the "pointee").\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # Read from pointee\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 ptr\cf5 [])\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb13 # mutate pointee\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 ptr\cf5 []\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 0\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 5\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you've allocated space for multiple values, you can use subscript syntax to access the values, as if they were an array, like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ptr[3]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The empty subscript 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 []
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has the same meaning as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 [0]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf19 \cb20 Caution\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb20 The dereference operator assumes that the memory being dereferenced is initialized. Dereferencing uninitialized memory results in undefined behavior.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You cannot safely use the dereference operator on uninitialized memory, even to initialize a pointee. This is because assigning to a dereferenced pointer calls lifecycle methods on the existing pointee (such as the destructor, move constructor or copy constructor).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  str_ptr \cf5 =\cf14  alloc\cf5 [\cf14 String\cf5 ](\cf15 1\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb13 # str_ptr[] = "Testing" # Undefined behavior!\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 str_ptr\cf5 .\cf14 init_pointee_move\cf5 (\cf18 "Testing"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 str_ptr\cf5 []\cf14  \cf5 +=\cf14  \cf18 " pointers"\cf14  \cf6 # Works now\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Destroying or removing values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/#take_pointee"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul take_pointee()}} method moves a pointee from the memory location pointed to by 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ptr
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . This is a consuming move. It invokes 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__(take=)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  on the destination value. It leaves the memory location uninitialized.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/#destroy_pointee"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul destroy_pointee()}} method calls the destructor on the pointee, and leaves the memory location pointed to by 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ptr
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  uninitialized.\
Both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 take_pointee()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 destroy_pointee()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  require that the pointer is non-null, and the memory location contains a valid, initialized value of the pointee's type; otherwise the function results in undefined behavior.\
Calling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/#init_pointee_move_from"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul init_pointee_move_from(self, src)}} moves the value pointed to by 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 src
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  into the memory location pointed to by 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . After this operation, ownership of that value transfers from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 src
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and the memory at 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 src
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is uninitialized: do not read from it, and do not invoke destructors on it. To make the memory valid again, initialize it with a new value using one of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 init_pointee_*()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operations.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb21 Mojo assumes the destination memory is uninitialized. It does not destroy existing contents before writing the value from 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb22 src
\fs28 \cf2 \cb21 .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Freeing memory\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Calling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/#free"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul free()}} on a pointer frees the memory allocated by the pointer. It doesn't call the destructors on any values stored in the memory\'97you need to do that explicitly (for example, using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/#destroy_pointee"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul destroy_pointee()}} or one of the other functions described in \cf5 \ul Destroying or removing values\cf2 \ulnone ).\
Disposing of a pointer without freeing the associated memory can result in a memory leak\'97where your program keeps taking more and more memory, because not all allocated memory is being freed.\
On the other hand, if you have multiple copies of a pointer accessing the same memory, you need to make sure you only call 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 free()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  on one of them. Freeing the same memory twice is also an error.\
After freeing a pointer's memory, you're left with a dangling pointer\'97its address still points to the freed memory. Any attempt to access the memory, like dereferencing the pointer results in undefined behavior.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Storing multiple values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As mentioned in \cf5 \ul Allocating memory\cf2 \ulnone , you can use an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to allocate memory for multiple values. The memory is allocated as a single, contiguous block. Pointers support arithmetic: adding an integer to a pointer returns a new pointer offset by the specified number of values from the original pointer:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  third_ptr \cf5 =\cf14  first_ptr \cf5 +\cf14  \cf15 2\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Pointers also support subtraction, as well as in-place addition and subtraction:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # Advance the pointer one element:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 ptr \cf5 +=\cf14  \cf15 1\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 3. Pointer arithmetic\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 For example, the following example allocates memory to store 6 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values, and initializes them all to zero.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  float_ptr \cf5 =\cf14  alloc\cf5 [\cf14 Float64\cf5 ](\cf15 6\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 for\cf14  offset \cf5 in\cf14  \cf23 range\cf5 (\cf15 6\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 (\cf14 float_ptr\cf5 +\cf14 offset\cf5 ).\cf14 init_pointee_copy\cf5 (\cf15 0.0\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Once the values are initialized, you can access them using subscript syntax:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 float_ptr\cf5 [\cf15 2\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 3.0\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 for\cf14  offset \cf5 in\cf14  \cf23 range\cf5 (\cf15 6\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 float_ptr\cf5 [\cf14 offset\cf5 ],\cf14  end\cf5 =\cf18 ", "\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
