\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 0.0, 0.0, 3.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0,\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  and origins\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct has an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 origin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter to track the origin of the memory it points to.\
For pointers initialized with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 to
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword argument, the origin is set to the origin of the pointee. For example, in the following code, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 s_ptr.origin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is the same as the origin of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 s
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 s \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 "Testing"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 s_ptr \cf5 =\cf14  UnsafePointer\cf5 (\cf14 to\cf5 =\cf14 s\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When allocating memory with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 alloc()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function, the returned pointer has an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 origin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MutExternalOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . This value represents an origin that is mutable and doesn't alias existing values. For example, it doesn't point to the memory allocated for any other variable. That means the compiler won't unnecessarily extend the lifetimes of unrelated values. This memory isn't tracked by Mojo's lifetime checker and you're responsible for freeing it.\
If you're using a pointer in the implementation of a struct, you usually don't have to worry about the origin, as long as the pointer isn't exposed outside of of the struct. For example, if you implement a static array type that allocates memory in its constructor, deallocates it in its destructor, and doesn't expose the pointer outside of the struct, the default origin is fine.\
But if the struct exposes a pointer to that memory, you need to set the origin appropriately. For example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type has an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 unsafe_ptr()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that returns an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to the underlying storage. In this case, the returned pointer should share the origin of the list, since the list is the logical owner of the storage.\
That method looks something like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf23 unsafe_ptr\cf5 (\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 ref\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  UnsafePointer\cf5 [\cf14 T\cf5 ,\cf14  origin_of\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 )]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 data\cf5 .\cf14 unsafe_origin_cast\cf5 [\cf14 origin_of\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 )]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This returns a copy of the original pointer, with the origin set to match the origin and mutability of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value.\
A method like this is unsafe, but setting the correct origin makes it safer, since the compiler knows that the pointer is referring to data owned by the list.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Working with foreign pointers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When exchanging data with other programming languages, you may need to construct an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  from a foreign pointer. Mojo restricts creating 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instances from arbitrary addresses, to avoid users accidentally creating pointers that alias each other (that is, two pointers that refer to the same location). However, there are specific methods you can use to get an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  from a Python or C/C++ pointer.\
When dealing with memory allocated elsewhere, you need to be aware of who's responsible for freeing the memory. Freeing memory allocated elsewhere can result in undefined behavior.\
When working with some foreign functions, you may need to supply a pointer with no specific type (a type-erased pointer, or "void pointer" in C/C++). This is equivalent to a Mojo 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OpaquePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
You also need to be aware of the format of the data stored in memory, including data types and byte order. For more information, see \cf5 \ul Converting data: bitcasting and byte order\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Creating a Mojo pointer from a raw memory address\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  from a raw memory address using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 unsafe_from_address
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  initializer.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf23 write_to_address\cf5 (\cf14 mmio_address\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  Int32\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  ptr \cf5 =\cf14  UnsafePointer\cf5 [\cf14 Int32\cf5 ,\cf14  MutExternalOrigin\cf5 ](\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13       unsafe_from_address\cf5 =\cf14 mmio_address\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13      \cf6 # Writing to a raw memory address may require a volatile load/store as the\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13      \cf6 # operation may have side effects not visible to the compiler.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13      \cf6 # You can specify this using the `volatile` parameter.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     ptr\cf5 .\cf14 store\cf5 [\cf14 volatile \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 True\cf5 ](\cf14 value\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This is unsafe, as the caller must ensure the address is valid before writing to it, and that the memory is initialized before reading from it. The caller must also ensure the pointer's origin and mutability is valid for the address, failure to to do may result in undefined behavior.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Creating a Mojo pointer from a Python pointer\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type defines an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python_object/PythonObject/#unsafe_get_as_pointer"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul unsafe_get_as_pointer()}} method to construct an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  from a Python address.\
For example, the following code creates a NumPy array and then accesses the data using a Mojo pointer:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 python \cf5 import\cf14  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf23 share_array\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     np \cf5 =\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf14 import_module\cf5 (\cf18 "numpy"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     arr \cf5 =\cf14  np\cf5 .\cf14 array\cf5 (\cf14 Python\cf5 .\cf23 list\cf5 (\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 3\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 4\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 5\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 6\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 7\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 8\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 9\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     ptr \cf5 =\cf14  arr\cf5 .\cf14 ctypes\cf5 .\cf14 data\cf5 .\cf14 unsafe_get_as_pointer\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 int64\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf23 range\cf5 (\cf15 9\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 ptr\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ],\cf14  end\cf5 =\cf18 ", "\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print()\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf23 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     share_array\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9,\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This example uses the NumPy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.ndarray.ctypes.html#numpy.ndarray.ctypes"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul ndarray.ctype}} attribute to access the raw pointer to the underlying storage (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ndarray.ctype.data
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 unsafe_get_as_pointer()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method constructs an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to this address.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Working with C/C++ pointers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you call a C/C++ function that returns a pointer using the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/sys/ffi/external_call/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul external_call}} function, you can specify the return type as an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and Mojo will handle the type conversion for you.\
Notably, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 origin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter when working across FFI boundaries should often be set to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 (Mut/Immut)ExternalOrigin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , since the pointer points to memory external to the mojo program.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 ffi \cf5 import\cf14  external_call\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf23 get_foreign_pointer\cf5 ()\cf14  raises \cf5 ->\cf14  UnsafePointer\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ,\cf14  MutExternalOrigin\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  ptr \cf5 =\cf14  external_call\cf5 [\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf18 "my_c_function"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf6 # external function name\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         UnsafePointer\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ,\cf14  MutExternalOrigin\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf6 # return type\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  ptr\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Opaque pointers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OpaquePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type is a pointer that does not have a specific type. In other languages, this is usually called a type-erased pointer or a void pointer. Opaque pointers are usually used when interfacing with non-Mojo code, such as a C library function that takes a void pointer.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OpaquePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is actually a type alias for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer[NoneType]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , so it has the same API as any other 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
You can't dereference an opaque pointer, but you can cast it to a specific type using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 bitcast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method. Similarly, you can create an opaque pointer from an existing pointer by bitcasting to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 NoneType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  \cf23 str\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 "Hello, world!"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  str_ptr \cf5 =\cf14  UnsafePointer\cf5 (\cf14 to\cf5 =\cf23 str\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  opaque_ptr \cf5 =\cf14  str_ptr\cf5 .\cf14 bitcast\cf5 [\cf14 NoneType\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb13 # ... call some foreign function that takes a void pointer\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Converting data: bitcasting and byte order\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Bitcasting a pointer returns a new pointer that has the same memory location, but a new data type. This can be useful if you need to access different types of data from a single area of memory. This can happen when you're reading binary files, like image files, or receiving data over the network.\
The following sample processes a format that consists of chunks of data, where each chunk contains a variable number of 32-bit integers. Each chunk begins with an 8-bit integer that identifies the number of values in the chunk.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf23 read_chunks\cf5 (\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 var\cf14  ptr\cf5 :\cf14  UnsafePointer\cf5 [mut=\cf15 False\cf5 ,\cf14  UInt8\cf5 ],\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 )\cf14  raises \cf5 ->\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 List\cf5 [\cf14 UInt32\cf5 ]]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     chunks \cf5 =\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 List\cf5 [\cf14 UInt32\cf5 ]]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # A chunk size of 0 indicates the end of the data\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     chunk_size \cf5 =\cf14  Int\cf5 (\cf14 ptr\cf5 [])\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 while\cf14  \cf5 (\cf14 chunk_size \cf5 >\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # Skip the 1 byte chunk_size and get a pointer to the first\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # UInt32 in the chunk\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         ui32_ptr \cf5 =\cf14  \cf5 (\cf14 ptr \cf5 +\cf14  \cf15 1\cf5 ).\cf14 bitcast\cf5 [\cf14 UInt32\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         chunk \cf5 =\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 UInt32\cf5 ](\cf14 capacity\cf5 =\cf14 chunk_size\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf23 range\cf5 (\cf14 chunk_size\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             chunk\cf5 .\cf14 append\cf5 (\cf14 ui32_ptr\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ])\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         chunks\cf5 .\cf14 append\cf5 (\cf14 chunk\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # Move our pointer to the next byte after the current chunk\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         ptr \cf5 +=\cf14  \cf5 (\cf15 1\cf14  \cf5 +\cf14  \cf15 4\cf14  \cf5 *\cf14  chunk_size\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # Read the size of the next chunk\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         chunk_size \cf5 =\cf14  Int\cf5 (\cf14 ptr\cf5 [])\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  chunks\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

