\fs64 \cf2 Working with SIMD vectors\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type includes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/#load"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul load()}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/#store"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul store()}} methods for performing aligned loads and stores of scalar values. It also has methods supporting strided load/store and gather/scatter.\
Strided load loads values from memory into a SIMD vector using an offset (the "stride") between successive memory addresses. This can be useful for extracting rows or columns from tabular data, or for extracting individual values from structured data. For example, consider the data for an RGB image, where each pixel is made up of three 8-bit values, for red, green, and blue. If you want to access just the red values, you can use a strided load or store.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
