\cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/#gather"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul gather()}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/memory/unsafe_pointer/UnsafePointer/#scatter"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul scatter()}} methods let you load or store a set of values that are stored in arbitrary locations. You do this by passing in a SIMD vector of offsets to the current pointer. For example, when using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 gather()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the nth value in the vector is loaded from (pointer address) + offset[n].\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Safety\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Unsafe pointers are unsafe for several reasons:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Memory management is up to the user. You need to manually allocate and free memory, and/or be aware of when other APIs are allocating or freeing memory for you.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values are nullable\'97that is, the pointer is not guaranteed to point to anything. And even when a pointer points to allocated memory, that memory may not be initialized.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  does have an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 origin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter so Mojo can track the origin of the data it points to, but it also provides unsafe APIs. For example, when you do pointer arithmetic, the compiler doesn't do any bounds checking.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri960\partightenfactor0
